Q: How to force java to show full stack trace A: <p>Java does not truncate stack traces by default. If you’re not seeing the full stack trace, it’s usually due to one of the following:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>The exception is being logged incorrectly</p>
</li>
<li><p>Only the message is printed</p>
</li>
<li><p>The logging framework is configured to shorten output</p>
</li>
<li><p>You are not printing the root cause</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>To ensure the full stack trace is printed, do this:</p>
<pre><code>try {
    // code
} catch (Exception e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you're using a logging framework (Log4j, SLF4J, etc.), make sure you pass the exception object:</p>
<pre><code>logger.error(&quot;Unexpected error occurred&quot;, e);
</code></pre>
<p>Do <strong>not</strong> do this:</p>
<pre><code>logger.error(e.getMessage()); // prints only the message
</code></pre>
<p>If you need the complete stack trace as a string:</p>
<pre><code>import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.io.StringWriter;

StringWriter sw = new StringWriter();
PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(sw);
e.printStackTrace(pw);
String fullStackTrace = sw.toString();
</code></pre>
<p>If nested exceptions are involved, check the cause chain:</p>
<pre><code>Throwable cause = e;
while (cause != null) {
    cause.printStackTrace();
    cause = cause.getCause();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Also note that <code>StackOverflowError</code> is an <code>Error</code>, not an <code>Exception</code>, so catch it separately if necessary:</p>
<pre><code>catch (StackOverflowError e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
</code></pre>
<p>If the issue is related to deep recursion, you may need to increase the JVM stack size:</p>
<pre><code>-Xss2m
</code></pre>
